-5013-a35b-ba52c5b54bbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-18004" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/d43fa0efcfb8788316c86958b1175741c136588e81a8119131d1ca0f8d8ee229.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of four line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of four parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,-2),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-2,-1),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1),$ and the line segments for $x\\in [1,3].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{4}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,-2).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,-1).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{4}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}-{A}_{4} \\\\  & =1-1+2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-18004/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-18004"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-353385/selection/d4a22aed69ccaac0732ea6ccd67e782dbdb55eb818c0e13d739504088f5ccc9b" :answer/id #uuid "96e5fb65-a474-57f3-bdf0-2f33c9f3e495" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4+\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353385/selection/8e2fc53c4973b30c03d0a0738957ab9e809c481479e6682e55708983ca8fe1b5" :answer/id #uuid "406edace-aa97-5a5b-8520-10002d54d74f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4+2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353385/selection/ffc410304b5884ae7ea67367063ca895011a836e2f68f7fdeec3c55bb0e5cb7c" :answer/id #uuid "b232b43b-2b81-5bbd-824b-da1e2bb0fd06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8+4\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353385/selection/9568beca2f83a86c040c7fc2b8084cc1ef819bd679f61151b98c03604c6992bf" :answer/id #uuid "73ea5a8d-7dfa-5d26-b577-81b32d7beaf6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8+\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353385/selection/b667c9648e0f4fb0f104f377a838d001bfc113bbe414a8cf9fc8044e61a1ad6c" :answer/id #uuid "a6bcaa03-156b-5139-8610-76ed1d6d7e81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8+2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353385/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "323901aa-4529-5a40-af1b-221b7a49f6de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-353385/selection/b667c9648e0f4fb0f104f377a838d001bfc113bbe414a8cf9fc8044e61a1ad6c" :answer-field/choices ["q-353385/selection/d4a22aed69ccaac0732ea6ccd67e782dbdb55eb818c0e13d739504088f5ccc9b" "q-353385/selection/8e2fc53c4973b30c03d0a0738957ab9e809c481479e6682e55708983ca8fe1b5" "q-353385/selection/ffc410304b5884ae7ea67367063ca895011a836e2f68f7fdeec3c55bb0e5cb7c" "q-353385/selection/9568beca2f83a86c040c7fc2b8084cc1ef819bd679f61151b98c03604c6992bf" "q-353385/selection/b667c9648e0f4fb0f104f377a838d001bfc113bbe414a8cf9fc8044e61a1ad6c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353385" :question/id #uuid "348b9583-e8e1-52b7-9364-b3b56e2e0bcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-353385" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/aa21323548eb2147949bac5bb379ed8fa6672d1b9fcb73137fad484c47e3cac9.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,2)$ and the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [2,6].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,2).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}b\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,6].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =8+2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-353385/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353385"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339328/field-1/dc5912e81a8d47c10c023b4a56ab75b4b4f2c5708b51f85634935f69a559be29" :answer/id #uuid "e7ba8acc-7bc1-58fe-b4c0-c7b3ab5c8913" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2+\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339328/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5621bfe6-26a6-5dc6-8aa5-44d378722e21" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-339328/field-1/dc5912e81a8d47c10c023b4a56ab75b4b4f2c5708b51f85634935f69a559be29" :answer-field/choices ["q-339328/field-1/dc5912e81a8d47c10c023b4a56ab75b4b4f2c5708b51f85634935f69a559be29"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339328" :question/id #uuid "a3181657-4385-5db5-a4d1-ee05b6d0a850" :question/math-academy-id "q-339328" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/dcc3b282b99d1b74669ea47455f424361258e33497527bd69e29d50c5083aec4.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-3),$ the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in (-3,-1),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [-1,4].$ Using the additive property of integrals, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-3).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-4.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-3,-1).$ The corresponding region is a combination of an inverted quarter-circle of radius $2$ and a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=\\pi -4.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,4].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =-4+\\pi -4+10 \\\\  & =2+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-339328/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339328"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-86174"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/3e0eca1a04396c0c86bbf5c3e5a9b3ff7071eacfe3d8c4281549e70cda809e8c.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-353387/selection/c357e6862530faa72ca0d68ad19a73c1e529aca9aa9b841a1f3dc13ec0b25f13" :answer/id #uuid "b7fb9a25-3870-5d8e-9e54-d2c4d0f5d3fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi +4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353387/selection/e024093d8b914ed64aa0dbd00c65e64b8d25bfae7cb7fa99234bf2e901bba0e2" :answer/id #uuid "102e1f8a-f770-53ca-91bf-9d06ba760e7a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi +8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353387/selection/b07d753c0433baf2ea68566eace39249123a6a251dbd58a9b0d9be7b02c3449b" :answer/id #uuid "891c1d62-d5a1-5127-b5aa-b14807a51b4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi +4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353387/selection/2410ac92630841717c5ed725730de777b29e76846e6404b6d769799945bdec9e" :answer/id #uuid "cd8038c8-1046-58e8-84f0-5162159f0c32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi +16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353387/selection/a39cd5786de55dee022debec1bc6d10a9d7990e874032a509feca954a25ada36" :answer/id #uuid "48776417-a438-5f07-b0e6-d31ef8f8314d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi +8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353387/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7a261122-1a15-5bfa-8e2c-cf52c60b75a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-353387/selection/a39cd5786de55dee022debec1bc6d10a9d7990e874032a509feca954a25ada36" :answer-field/choices ["q-353387/selection/c357e6862530faa72ca0d68ad19a73c1e529aca9aa9b841a1f3dc13ec0b25f13" "q-353387/selection/e024093d8b914ed64aa0dbd00c65e64b8d25bfae7cb7fa99234bf2e901bba0e2" "q-353387/selection/b07d753c0433baf2ea68566eace39249123a6a251dbd58a9b0d9be7b02c3449b" "q-353387/selection/2410ac92630841717c5ed725730de777b29e76846e6404b6d769799945bdec9e" "q-353387/selection/a39cd5786de55dee022debec1bc6d10a9d7990e874032a509feca954a25ada36"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353387" :question/id #uuid "97f5c0c0-1b45-5f58-be76-fdc17942bba8" :question/math-academy-id "q-353387" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/877771be920838a330edabe84b07eb817f1f3d7f1343b6866b9c2f078e079a67.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0)$ and the line segment for $x\\in [0,4].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0).$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}b\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-353387/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353387"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339573/field-1/a5d692470abab8da0b4654af4eadaff115a9cb991251309de88702bf59f6c7ac" :answer/id #uuid "1a738a47-cd39-59a2-b5ac-361680b66774" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}+2\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339573/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6bdd0db3-f8c7-52d3-b149-84b53d46f5de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-339573/field-1/a5d692470abab8da0b4654af4eadaff115a9cb991251309de88702bf59f6c7ac" :answer-field/choices ["q-339573/field-1/a5d692470abab8da0b4654af4eadaff115a9cb991251309de88702bf59f6c7ac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339573" :question/id #uuid "6bc413dd-9ad2-5c62-b6c1-bf39c8ac810b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339573" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/be0329f2bc3722a6b3381dcfdbed212dc1e647f7f165cefb4c00f939b74b6d74.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-3,1),$ the line segment for $x\\in [1,3],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [3,6].$ Therefore, we can use the additive property of integrals to write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,1).$   The corresponding region is a combination of a semi-circle of radius $2$ and a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (2)^{2}+4(1) \\\\  & =2\\pi +4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$   The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,6].$   The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi +4+2-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi +3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-339573/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339573"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-433"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/ad45cbdcd126df931f1a35c60ca12b45d520a2a0a5cddf4fa012c0515d3024c7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-434"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/36c6a1c84e0b0c32a5fd35fd422645f60340da6d205dd8cc31054cd558c2f5f9.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-4}^4 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
